 ## 5. Target Design
 
-The current accepted target is deterministic LRU with hit, miss, and eviction counters.
+No implementation change is requested at this stage. The design remains open pending benchmark evidence.
+
+## 5.1 Candidate Options
+
+- **A — LRU + TTL:** retain least-recently-used eviction and add per-entry time-to-live expiration. Compare how expiration interacts with recency and bounded memory.
+- **B — Segmented LRU:** divide entries into probationary and protected segments so frequently reused entries receive stronger retention than one-pass entries.
+- **C — TinyLFU admission + LRU eviction:** use TinyLFU frequency estimates to decide admission, then use LRU for eviction among admitted entries.
+
+## 5.2 Comparison Metrics
+
+Evaluate all candidates under the same capacity, workload traces, and measurement window using:
+
+- hit rate and miss rate;
+- eviction rate and admission/rejection rate;
+- stale-expiration rate (where applicable);
+- memory footprint and metadata overhead;
+- request latency, including policy decision overhead;
+- scan resistance and sensitivity to workload mix; and
+- operational complexity and observability.
+
+**Decision checkpoint:** measure with a representative load test and decide only after the benchmark results are available. 等压测后决定。
 
